#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162636 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162009 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4105f1cb-eb48-5946-92f7-cd5e67689912" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type by Calculating the Corresponding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82db51ea-bf33-5d31-8dfc-545569cf5119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161109} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba92099-726a-501d-80b0-a3110caa2f90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161110} {:answer/id #uuid "b2141f45-3669-53cb-993e-e53d7e5ab19e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161111} {:answer/id #uuid "063200f7-853c-54c9-90be-4e08e7fffe29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161112} {:answer/id #uuid "7e65e212-da6b-5bf3-be79-a15c98aee59d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e65e212-da6b-5bf3-be79-a15c98aee59d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161113} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5cef2fe-6358-5dbf-81eb-a5f9c43d0635" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a87a7ac-21af-54b9-b7c7-9dfc14e551b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-94464" :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are negative semi-definite?\n\nI. ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-3$\n\nIII. ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is *not* negative semi-definite since some of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are positive (for example, ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ is positive).\n\n- Quadratic form II is negative semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $R$ are non-positive.\n\n- Quadratic form III is negative semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $S$ are non-positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162638 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162010 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbebdd1b-0716-55cb-8df6-12a35b517ab5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd7ccdba-167d-52d3-9f65-006af58a47df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448792} {:answer/id #uuid "45e79804-304f-56d7-91a8-584e5bc05ec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448793}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45e79804-304f-56d7-91a8-584e5bc05ec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448793} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fe36113-bf8a-58df-bd27-b2358ccf9634" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449436} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "695c1734-15fe-5bd0-a319-a113359ef2ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448794} {:answer/id #uuid "bb89c867-cc14-5d01-bdda-46476f5b4ce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb89c867-cc14-5d01-bdda-46476f5b4ce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448795} :answer-field/id #uuid "83b596d7-227d-54aa-8cfb-eae069e9bd74" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449437} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf09e997-4a5d-5a14-a4c3-ad0a1321872f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448796} {:answer/id #uuid "09ac96d0-da48-5e6a-a5b5-c11c8827e02c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf09e997-4a5d-5a14-a4c3-ad0a1321872f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448796} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dba44f8-b993-5e8f-8c33-a9da0905d35e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a27d2cf-0561-5715-9600-290d8804741e" :question/math-academy-id "q-94466" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${a}_{11}=9$ and $det(A)=18$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- **III.** $A$ is negative-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=9$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}|=18.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=9>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}|=18>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525025 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162009 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4105f1cb-eb48-5946-92f7-cd5e67689912" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type by Calculating the Corresponding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12d6028b-8c3e-5aba-826a-b147b99e2f9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524416} {:answer/id #uuid "1b4abab5-df3e-5347-99fb-84fa6de03a5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524432} {:answer/id #uuid "2274a1ba-cb09-551d-a264-6a5d4c0e37fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524443} {:answer/id #uuid "2dffc13b-109f-56f9-902b-d52e5de34dcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524464} {:answer/id #uuid "bca55a76-d282-5f41-9c77-abe46819afe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12d6028b-8c3e-5aba-826a-b147b99e2f9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524416} :answer-field/id #uuid "feddc0fa-b88d-5ca9-bd43-99d017ae0768" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aa5d7a51-2b25-530e-bc34-f2b29d7db7d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-94788" :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{3}$ are negative-definite?\n\nI. ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-9$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-5$\n\nIII. ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=10$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-3$" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn. \n\n- Quadratic form I is negative-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are negative.\n\n- Quadratic form II is *not* negative-definite since not all of the eigenvalues of $R$ are negative (for example, ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$ is not negative).\n\n- Quadratic form III is *not* negative-definite since not all of the eigenvalues of $S$ are negative (${\\lambda }_{2}=10$ is not negative).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162009 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4105f1cb-eb48-5946-92f7-cd5e67689912" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type by Calculating the Corresponding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "342fba80-10c2-55b1-8abf-7ff9c9e264ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524476} {:answer/id #uuid "34e801a5-3875-5ecd-b05f-15545a5c3b9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524480} {:answer/id #uuid "4c8fe2d0-662b-51dd-836f-b0095a0df130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524512} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a717d0-ad06-5979-9d2e-d3fb8cedc6ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524645} {:answer/id #uuid "df3ce743-bd9f-55c4-aa50-daa5ef48006d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34e801a5-3875-5ecd-b05f-15545a5c3b9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524480} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc3a16ca-ab9d-5910-8403-1994cf7ec17a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "95072baf-8f6f-5ac9-b278-bac347e84d65" :question/math-academy-id "q-94796" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is positive-definite" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-5-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-5-\\lambda )(-5-\\lambda )-1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +24 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-6,\\,-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524999 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162010 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbebdd1b-0716-55cb-8df6-12a35b517ab5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c770309-60dd-57d9-8388-da614d588229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524434} {:answer/id #uuid "3543723c-355b-52c7-b10b-104509d59ebd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524481} {:answer/id #uuid "5585210e-e5b7-562b-96ab-a025b6173eec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524530} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ff6a24-e096-5a38-adf3-8f6baa63dc90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524638} {:answer/id #uuid "f936acee-b9ba-5a2d-aab8-0d02b6a64ffc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f936acee-b9ba-5a2d-aab8-0d02b6a64ffc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524827} :answer-field/id #uuid "079db36a-f121-5453-be8e-d40d62051237" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7aa930b4-3c77-5008-aaf5-9bbf298703c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-94800" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=-7$ and $det(A)=-3$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is positive-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-7$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|=3.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-7<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|=3>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
